WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Nereus connects fine-grained underwater attributes with spatially grounded counting.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
A dataset and evaluation resource for asking not only what is present, but what the image supports.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Underwater scenes combine clutter, camouflage and densely overlapping organisms.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.000
A category name does not describe morphology, condition or interaction.

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
A total count does not explain where organisms gather or how their surroundings relate to that pattern.

6
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:38.000
The manuscript reports 88,555 images and approximately 2.68 million question-answer pairs.

7
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:46.000
The schema spans 136 attribute dimensions. Fields are organism-specific, not universal to every object.

8
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Heterogeneous underwater sources are organized into object-centered and point-grounded supervision.

9
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:02.000
Object crops, image context and biological priors support multi-model attribute discussion and arbitration.

10
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Point annotations support region counts, visibility labels, density maps and evidence-linked questions.

11
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:19.000
Annotation combines model-assisted generation with targeted human review and expert calibration.

12
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Quality ratings and selected-case arbitration are not a whole-dataset accuracy estimate.

13
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:38.000
A localized fish example links rough texture, high camouflage and a hiding-in-coral interaction label.

14
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:48.000
The cuttlefish record describes mottled skin, high camouflage and a resting posture.

15
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:58.000
The coral record combines fan-like growth, intact tissue and a coral-rock contact label.

16
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Region-aware counting links each annotated fish to image coordinates and spatial cells.

17
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:11.000
Sample 0000 contains 90 annotated fish. Its densest grid cell is top-right, with 31.

18
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:22.000
Semantic memberships include 82 open-water and 5 reef-rock labels. Region memberships can overlap.

19
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Camouflage-state counting separates clear, ambiguous and highly camouflaged annotations.

20
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:38.000
Sample 0001 has 134 clear, 130 ambiguous and 6 highly camouflaged point annotations.

21
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:41.000
The close-up shows source-image evidence for highly camouflaged annotations.

22
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.000
Colored points are annotation labels, not model predictions.

23
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:51.000
Density-hotspot reasoning starts from a localized response in a supplied annotation-derived heatmap.

24
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:58.000
In open water, the annotation relates aggregation to available swimming space and possible schooling.

25
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:05.000
Near branching coral, visible habitat structure grounds the description of the fish aggregation.

26
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Shelter and food are annotation hypotheses, not verified causes. Depth labels are relative, not metric.

27
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:18.000
Track A uses RGB only. Track B additionally supplies a ground-truth-derived density heatmap.

28
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.000
Count error, relative count accuracy and grounded reasoning measure different capabilities.

29
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:33.000
Table 2 reports higher attribute accuracy after Nereus supervision for all three open backbones shown.

30
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:42.000
For InternVL2.5-8B, Table 3A reports lower count MAE and higher densest-cell accuracy under QAS.

31
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:50.000
Use the stated input conditions and source licenses. Ecological interpretations require domain validation.

32
00:03:50.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Explore the data, research code and interactive examples on the Nereus project page.
